This Java code block is part of the `PaymentGateway` class, specifically the method for processing credit card transactions. It includes error handling for common issues like invalid card numbers or insufficient funds.

```java
public boolean processCreditCard(PaymentDetails details) throws PaymentException {
    // Validate card number and expiry date
    if (!isValidCardNumber(details.getCardNumber())) {
        throw new PaymentException("Invalid credit card number.");
    }
    if (!isValidExpiryDate(details.getExpiryMonth(), details.getExpiryYear())) {
        throw new PaymentException("Invalid expiry date.");
    }

    // Attempt to authorize the transaction with the payment provider
    try {
        PaymentProvider.authorize(details.getAmount(), details.getToken());
        log.info("Credit card transaction authorized successfully.");
        return true;
    } catch (AuthorizationFailedException e) {
        log.error("Credit card authorization failed: " + e.getMessage());
        throw new PaymentException("Payment authorization failed.", e);
    }
}
```

The `PaymentDetails` object contains all necessary information including the amount and a secure token. We aim to abstract away the complexities of different payment providers. The exception handling ensures robustness in our payment processing.

The following Go code is from our `inventory_management` service. It handles the decrementing of stock levels when an order is fulfilled.

```go
func (im *InventoryManager) DecrementStock(productID string, quantity int) error {
    // Check if the product exists in our inventory system
    product, err := im.db.FindProductByID(productID)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("product %s not found: %w", productID, err)
    }

    // Ensure sufficient stock before decrementing
    if product.StockCount < quantity {
        return fmt.Errorf("insufficient stock for product %s: available %d, requested %d", productID, product.StockCount, quantity)
    }

    // Update the stock count in the database
    newStockCount := product.StockCount - quantity
    err = im.db.UpdateStock(productID, newStockCount)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("failed to update stock for product %s: %w", productID, err)
    }

    log.Printf("Successfully decremented stock for product %s by %d units.", productID, quantity)
    return nil
}
```

This function is called after an order has been successfully processed and payment confirmed. It ensures that our inventory levels remain accurate. We use a mutex to protect concurrent access to stock levels.

This C# code snippet is for our `CustomerService` class, specifically the method that updates a customer's profile information. It includes basic input sanitization.

```csharp
public bool UpdateCustomerProfile(int customerId, CustomerProfileData profileData)
{
    // Basic validation for required fields
    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(profileData.FirstName) || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(profileData.LastName))
    {
        log.LogError("Customer {CustomerId}: First name and last name are required.", customerId);
        return false;
    }

    // Sanitize potentially harmful input (e.g., script tags)
    profileData.Bio = SanitizeHtml(profileData.Bio);

    // Update the customer record in the database
    bool success = _customerRepository.Update(customerId, profileData);
    if (success)
    {
        log.LogInformation("Customer {CustomerId} profile updated successfully.", customerId);
    }
    else
    {
