This PHP code is from the `ProductController` and handles the creation of new product listings. It validates input data before saving it to the database.

```php
public function createProduct(Request $request)
{
    // Validate incoming product data
    $validator = Validator::make($request->all(), [
        'name' => 'required|string|max:255',
        'description' => 'nullable|string',
        'price' => 'required|numeric|min:0.01',
        'stock_quantity' => 'required|integer|min:0'
    ]);

    if ($validator->fails()) {
        return response()->json(['errors' => $validator->errors()], 422);
    }

    $productData = $validator->validated();
    $product = new Product($productData);

    // Attempt to save the new product to the database
    if ($product->save()) {
        log::info('New product created successfully: ' . $product->name);
        return response()->json($product, 201);
    } else {
        log::error('Failed to create new product.');
        return response()->json(['message' => 'Product creation failed.'], 500);
    }
}
```

This controller action ensures that all new products meet our defined schema before being added to the catalog. It leverages the framework's built-in validation features. Logging is included for both success and failure scenarios. The response includes appropriate HTTP status codes.

Here is a snippet of JavaScript code from our front-end application responsible for handling user profile updates. It makes an API call to our backend service.

```javascript
async function updateUserProfile(userId, profileData) {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(`/api/v1/users/${userId}/profile`, {
            method: 'PUT',
            headers: {
                'Content-Type': 'application/json',
                'Authorization': `Bearer ${getAuthToken()}` // Assuming getAuthToken retrieves the user's JWT
            },
            body: JSON.stringify(profileData)
        });

        if (!response.ok) {
            const errorData = await response.json();
            throw new Error(`API Error: ${response.status} - ${errorData.message}`);
        }

        const updatedProfile = await response.json();
        console.log(`User profile for ${userId} updated successfully.`);
        return updatedProfile;
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(`Failed to update user profile for ${userId}:`, error);
        // Potentially display an error message to the user
        return null;
    }
}
```

This function sends the profile data, including the user ID, via a PUT request to the appropriate API endpoint. It includes authentication headers using a JWT obtained from `getAuthToken()`. Error handling is in place to catch network issues or API-level errors. The `Authorization` header is critical for security.

